 "action_summary": "The owner asked, again, whether giving the model the smartest coding actions once teaches them permanently. Nine documents on main answer the COUNT half of that (one showing is literal, two showings generalise). This round measures the half the word SMARTEST actually names: given one showing, WHICH file is shown. On 15 merged pull requests of other people's projects (89 files, 15 tasks, hash-verified), the same change demonstrated on different files of the SAME task recovers between 0 and 100 in 100 of what the people there did; median spread 0.14 across 15 tasks, 10 of 15 non-zero. A rule that needs no outcome --- the reach of a candidate's own lesson, i.e. how many sibling candidate files it fits --- correlates r=0.88 (strict arm) and r=0.66 (ceiling arm) with what the demonstration will recover. Where that reach identifies ONE candidate the rule is right 3 of 3 (strict) and 3 of 3 (ceiling). Where several candidates tie at the greatest reach the rule does NOT decide, and the honest expectation there is the tie's mean, which is reported separately because breaking the tie by file path would credit the rule with luck. This is a measurement of a decision procedure, not a capability.",
